speechtext:
Anyway, the matter is somewhat clearer now than it was before. I must say that I thought I had given the information.- However, these details are sometimes missed because when one is answering hundreds of questions one can forget minor things. My recollection is that the hon. member asked for information concerning income tax forms in relation to employees in the Montreal and Ottawa post offices. Then, in one of his notes he mentioned Montreal and Toronto. My information is as follows:
In Toronto the forms were duly processed in the Toronto post office after receipt from the treasury office and were all released on March 8.
In Montreal, according to information received from the postmaster, the forms were released as follows:
T-4 forms for regular salaries for full-time staff, March 18;
T-4 forms for the night differential for the full-time staff, March 18;
T-4 forms for the salaries of the part-time staff, March 18;
T-4 forms for the Christmas help, March 21;
TP-4 forms for the regular salaries for the full-time staff, March 18;
TP-4 forms for night differential for the full-time staff, March 23;
TP-4 forms for salaries of the part-time staff and Christmas help, March 23.
In Ottawa, all forms were released between March 1 and March 4.
Prior to this year of course these TP-4 forms which are required for employees in Quebec were not necessary. Prior to this year the night differential was liquidated in time, that is, by hours off rather than in dollars and cents.
Then, one other point; there is a month's delay in figuring out the actual overtime and the amount that has to be paid to an individual for overtime. All this meant additional forms to be made and to be filled out. Apparently it was not possible to do this

Supply-Post Office
with the same size of staff as quickly as it was done in the previous years.
The answer is quite obvious: Either the regulation has to be changed or additional staff must be added to get them out that much earlier. I hope I have given the hon. member all the information he requires.
In reply to the hon. member for Nipissing, may I say I understand there is an agreement between the Department of National Defence and the Post Office Department whereby in those cases where the camps are what are generally known as closed camps the Department of National Defence supplies the staff; and in those camps known as "open" camps, the Post Office Department supplies the staff. However, may I suggest that in this particular case I shall be glad to look into the matter and try to give the full details next week.
In reply to the hon. member for Brandon-Souris, I would say there is a trend over a period of years for the number of rural routes to increase, for the mileage based on the daily service to increase, and for the number of rural mail boxes to increase. However, in changing times, with more people having motor cars, with more people moving off farms, with some roads not being kept up as well as they were, while others are being kept up better, there will, of course, be rearrangements in routes. Each case has to be studied on its own merits.
With regard to his question about suburban areas, I think the hon. member will find that his question was answered this afternoon.
I am sorry I cannot give any definite information regarding the problem at Virden, or the particular problem in Brandon. I think he knows however that I shall be very happy to look into both of them. I hope he will not expect me to give the information to him next week, because I am going to visit the province, of which he is so proud, and the week will be a short one so far as my stay in Ottawa is concerned.
